189
00:06:55,919 --> 00:06:59,599
Let's take a moment to look at some

190
00:06:57,520 --> 00:07:02,720
fundamental syntax in mat lab that

191
00:06:59,599 --> 00:07:05,120
you'll see all the time. The percent

192
00:07:02,720 --> 00:07:07,039
symbol is used for writing comments.

193
00:07:05,120 --> 00:07:09,120
Anything after that symbol is ignored by

194
00:07:07,039 --> 00:07:12,720
mat lab. So it's a great way to document

195
00:07:09,120 --> 00:07:14,560
your code. A semicolon tells mat lab not

196
00:07:12,720 --> 00:07:17,039
to display the output of a command which

197
00:07:14,560 --> 00:07:18,960
keeps your console cleaner.

198
00:07:17,039 --> 00:07:20,960
If a command is getting too long, you

199
00:07:18,960 --> 00:07:23,360
can break it across multiple lines using

200
00:07:20,960 --> 00:07:25,199
three dots. This helps keep your code

201
00:07:23,360 --> 00:07:27,199
readable.

202
00:07:25,199 --> 00:07:29,280
Mat Lab also includes a few important

203
00:07:27,199 --> 00:07:32,080
constants. EPS stands for machine

204
00:07:29,280 --> 00:07:34,400
precision. INF is infinity and nan which

205
00:07:32,080 --> 00:07:36,319
stands for not a number.

206
00:07:34,400 --> 00:07:38,479
It appears when a calculation isn't

207
00:07:36,319 --> 00:07:40,960
mathematically defined like dividing

208
00:07:38,479 --> 00:07:42,880
some number by zero.

209
00:07:40,960 --> 00:07:45,440
To control how numbers appear on your

210
00:07:42,880 --> 00:07:47,520
screen, use formatting commands. For

211
00:07:45,440 --> 00:07:50,560
example, format long shows more decimal

212
00:07:47,520 --> 00:07:52,479
places and format short shows fewer. You

213
00:07:50,560 --> 00:07:55,520
also have access to a large library of

214
00:07:52,479 --> 00:07:58,080
built-in functions like sqrt for square

215
00:07:55,520 --> 00:08:00,240
roots, exponential for exponentials and

216
00:07:58,080 --> 00:08:03,680
trigonometric functions such as sin and

217
00:08:00,240 --> 00:08:06,080
cos. Basic arithmetic is intuitive.

218
00:08:03,680 --> 00:08:08,639
You'll use plus minus times and divide

219
00:08:06,080 --> 00:08:10,879
symbols just as you'd expect. And for

220
00:08:08,639 --> 00:08:13,039
constants, mat lab gives you pi and

221
00:08:10,879 --> 00:08:16,319
eer's number which you can access with

222
00:08:13,039 --> 00:08:18,479
exponential one. Together these form the

223
00:08:16,319 --> 00:08:20,800
foundation of all numerical work in mat

224
00:08:18,479 --> 00:08:23,680
lab.

225
00:08:20,800 --> 00:08:28,879
Suppose we define V as a row vector

226
00:08:23,680 --> 00:08:31,360
equals -2 3 0 4.5 and -1.5.

227
00:08:28,879 --> 00:08:34,479
To make it a column, we write V equals V

228
00:08:31,360 --> 00:08:38,159
prime using the transpose. To access

229
00:08:34,479 --> 00:08:40,479
elements, V of 1 gives the first, V of 2

230
00:08:38,159 --> 00:08:43,519
to four gives the second to fourth, and

231
00:08:40,479 --> 00:08:46,000
V of three and 5 gives just those two.

232
00:08:43,519 --> 00:08:49,040
You can also create sequences. For

233
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:51,120
example, V= 4:2, stepping by negative 1

234
00:08:49,040 --> 00:08:54,800
gives 432.

235
00:08:51,120 --> 00:09:02,000
And combining vectors is easy. If A= 1:3

236
00:08:54,800 --> 00:09:03,519
and B= 2 3, then C= A B gives 1 2 3 2 3.

237
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:06,720
Let's see how to create and work with

238
00:09:03,519 --> 00:09:09,279
matrices in mat lab. To get evenly

239
00:09:06,720 --> 00:09:12,000
spaced numbers, use lindspace. For

240
00:09:09,279 --> 00:09:14,720
example, x equals lin space from minus

241
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:17,279
pi to pi with 10 points gives 10 values

242
00:09:14,720 --> 00:09:19,839
across that range. For logarithmic

243
00:09:17,279 --> 00:09:22,720
spacing, use log space.

244
00:09:19,839 --> 00:09:25,760
To define a matrix, write a equals

245
00:09:22,720 --> 00:09:30,640
square bracket 1 2 3 4 5 6. That gives a

246
00:09:25,760 --> 00:09:32,640
2x3 matrix. To access elements, a of 1 2

247
00:09:30,640 --> 00:09:35,440
gives the value in the first row, second

248
00:09:32,640 --> 00:09:37,920
column. A colon two returns the second

249
00:09:35,440 --> 00:09:39,279
column. A two colon gives the second

250
00:09:37,920 --> 00:09:41,839
row.

251
00:09:39,279 --> 00:09:44,720
transpose with a prime and get the

252
00:09:41,839 --> 00:09:47,760
determinant using determinant of a. You

253
00:09:44,720 --> 00:09:51,360
can add, subtract or scale matrices like

254
00:09:47,760 --> 00:09:54,240
a plus b or 2 * a. Use a star b for

255
00:09:51,360 --> 00:09:57,519
matrix multiplication and a dot star b

256
00:09:54,240 --> 00:09:59,680
for element by element.

257
00:09:57,519 --> 00:10:01,760
Mat lab also makes it easy to generate

258
00:09:59,680 --> 00:10:03,440
special purpose matrices which are often

259
00:10:01,760 --> 00:10:04,959
useful for setting up or testing

260
00:10:03,440 --> 00:10:06,880
algorithms.

261
00:10:04,959 --> 00:10:09,360
For example, diagonal of V creates a

262
00:10:06,880 --> 00:10:11,600
diagonal matrix from a vector. You can

263
00:10:09,360 --> 00:10:14,720
also reverse it. Diagonal of A pulls the

264
00:10:11,600 --> 00:10:16,800
diagonal elements from a matrix.

265
00:10:14,720 --> 00:10:18,640
If you need an identity matrix, that's a

266
00:10:16,800 --> 00:10:22,560
square matrix with ones on the diagonal

267
00:10:18,640 --> 00:10:24,480
and zeros elsewhere, just write I of N.

268
00:10:22,560 --> 00:10:27,040
To create a matrix full of zeros, use

269
00:10:24,480 --> 00:10:29,839
zeros of M and N. If you need one filled

270
00:10:27,040 --> 00:10:31,760
with ones, use ones of M and N.

271
00:10:29,839 --> 00:10:33,920
These kinds of matrices are especially

272
00:10:31,760 --> 00:10:37,839
useful when initializing arrays before

273
00:10:33,920 --> 00:10:39,760
filling them with computed values.

274
00:10:37,839 --> 00:10:42,560
Now let's look at logical operations in

275
00:10:39,760 --> 00:10:45,040
mat lab. You can compare values using

276
00:10:42,560 --> 00:10:46,800
operators like double equals less than

277
00:10:45,040 --> 00:10:49,279
greater than or not equals which is

278
00:10:46,800 --> 00:10:51,440
written as tilda equals.

279
00:10:49,279 --> 00:10:53,440
The tilda symbol means not. For

280
00:10:51,440 --> 00:10:57,040
combining conditions use amperand for

281
00:10:53,440 --> 00:10:58,720
and and vertical bar for o r. To find

282
00:10:57,040 --> 00:11:01,040
specific values in an array, use the

283
00:10:58,720 --> 00:11:03,519
find function. For example, find a

284
00:11:01,040 --> 00:11:05,839
equals 3 gives you the positions where a

285
00:11:03,519 --> 00:11:07,600
is exactly three.

286
00:11:05,839 --> 00:11:09,600
These logical tools are great for

287
00:11:07,600 --> 00:11:12,079
filtering data and writing conditional

288
00:11:09,600 --> 00:11:13,839
code.

289
00:11:12,079 --> 00:11:15,680
All right, let's shift gears and talk

290
00:11:13,839 --> 00:11:17,680
about solving systems of linear

291
00:11:15,680 --> 00:11:19,680
equations. Something that comes up all

292
00:11:17,680 --> 00:11:22,320
the time in engineering, physics, and

293
00:11:19,680 --> 00:11:24,079
yes, even in medical imaging.

294
00:11:22,320 --> 00:11:27,360
Suppose we're working with a system like

295
00:11:24,079 --> 00:11:28,959
a * xals b. Now you might think, okay,

296
00:11:27,360 --> 00:11:33,680
just take the inverse of a and multiply

297
00:11:28,959 --> 00:11:35,760
it by b. So x= inv * b.

298
00:11:33,680 --> 00:11:38,320
And sure, that's mathematically valid.

299
00:11:35,760 --> 00:11:40,560
But in practice, it's a bad idea. It's

300
00:11:38,320 --> 00:11:42,079
slow, it's unstable, and it can lead to

301
00:11:40,560 --> 00:11:44,000
all kinds of numerical issues,

302
00:11:42,079 --> 00:11:45,920
especially with large or illitioned

303
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:47,760
matrices.

304
00:11:45,920 --> 00:11:50,160
Instead, mat lab gives us a much better

305
00:11:47,760 --> 00:11:53,839
tool, the backslash operator. So we

306
00:11:50,160 --> 00:11:55,680
write x= a back slashb. This tells mat

307
00:11:53,839 --> 00:11:57,920
lab to choose the most efficient way to

308
00:11:55,680 --> 00:12:00,959
solve the system behind the scenes. No

309
00:11:57,920 --> 00:12:02,959
explicit inverse needed. Later on we'll

310
00:12:00,959 --> 00:12:04,959
learn how to implement our own solvers.

311
00:12:02,959 --> 00:12:07,519
But for now this built-in method is what

312
00:12:04,959 --> 00:12:11,120
you should use. It's clean, fast, and

313
00:12:07,519 --> 00:12:12,959
accurate. Everything we want.

314
00:12:11,120 --> 00:12:14,720
Now that we've seen how to work with

315
00:12:12,959 --> 00:12:17,040
equations, let's take a moment to

316
00:12:14,720 --> 00:12:19,600
explore how we can inspect the structure

317
00:12:17,040 --> 00:12:21,440
of the data. We're working with vectors

318
00:12:19,600 --> 00:12:23,440
and matrices.

319
00:12:21,440 --> 00:12:25,600
To find out how long a vector is, just

320
00:12:23,440 --> 00:12:28,160
use length. So length of V tells you how

321
00:12:25,600 --> 00:12:30,000
many elements it has. If you're working

322
00:12:28,160 --> 00:12:32,399
with a matrix and you want to know its

323
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:35,200
shape, how many rows and columns? Just

324
00:12:32,399 --> 00:12:38,079
use size of A. Want to check if a matrix

325
00:12:35,200 --> 00:12:40,560
is full rank? That's easy. Rank of A

326
00:12:38,079 --> 00:12:42,800
will do the job. And when we want to

327
00:12:40,560 --> 00:12:45,040
understand how large a matrix or vector

328
00:12:42,800 --> 00:12:47,760
is in terms of magnitude, we use the

329
00:12:45,040 --> 00:12:49,839
norm function. Just norm of a gives the

330
00:12:47,760 --> 00:12:52,720
two norm by default. That's the square

331
00:12:49,839 --> 00:12:54,959
root of the sum of squares. You can also

332
00:12:52,720 --> 00:12:57,360
ask for specific norms like the one norm

333
00:12:54,959 --> 00:13:00,560
using norm of a1 or the infinity norm

334
00:12:57,360 --> 00:13:02,560
with norm of a and f. These commands

335
00:13:00,560 --> 00:13:04,480
give you insight into the structure and

336
00:13:02,560 --> 00:13:06,320
stability of your data. And they're

337
00:13:04,480 --> 00:13:08,399
especially useful when you're preparing

338
00:13:06,320 --> 00:13:11,120
data for algorithms or interpreting

339
00:13:08,399 --> 00:13:12,720
results.

340
00:13:11,120 --> 00:13:15,360
Let's talk about control flow,

341
00:13:12,720 --> 00:13:17,360
specifically loops. Start with a for

342
00:13:15,360 --> 00:13:20,079
loop. Use it when you know how many

343
00:13:17,360 --> 00:13:23,839
times you want to repeat something.

344
00:13:20,079 --> 00:13:26,720
For example, set x to zero. Then for i

345
00:13:23,839 --> 00:13:30,240
from 1 to 5 in steps of two, so 1 3 and

346
00:13:26,720 --> 00:13:32,639
5, we add i to x. At the end, x equals

347
00:13:30,240 --> 00:13:35,279
9. For loops are useful when working

348
00:13:32,639 --> 00:13:37,680
through a known sequence.

349
00:13:35,279 --> 00:13:40,880
Now, a while loop runs as long as the

350
00:13:37,680 --> 00:13:44,000
condition is true. In this example,

351
00:13:40,880 --> 00:13:46,240
start with x equal to 7. While x is

352
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:48,959
greater than or equal to zero, subtract

353
00:13:46,240 --> 00:13:51,279
two each time. The loop stops when x

354
00:13:48,959 --> 00:13:53,120
becomes negative. Just be careful. If

355
00:13:51,279 --> 00:13:55,120
the condition never becomes false, the

356
00:13:53,120 --> 00:13:56,959
loop won't stop.

357
00:13:55,120 --> 00:13:59,519
Finally, break lets you exit a loop

358
00:13:56,959 --> 00:14:02,160
early. It's useful if you find what you

359
00:13:59,519 --> 00:14:04,800
need before the loop finishes. In nested

360
00:14:02,160 --> 00:14:07,040
loops, it only exits the innermost one.

361
00:14:04,800 --> 00:14:09,760
Use it wisely. Don't skip steps that

362
00:14:07,040 --> 00:14:11,279
still matter.

363
00:14:09,760 --> 00:14:15,120
Let's talk about how we make decisions

364
00:14:11,279 --> 00:14:17,920
in mat lab. First the if statement. You

365
00:14:15,120 --> 00:14:20,800
might say if x is equal to 3, display

366
00:14:17,920 --> 00:14:23,760
the value of x is three. If x is equal

367
00:14:20,800 --> 00:14:26,000
to 5, display the value of x is 5.

368
00:14:23,760 --> 00:14:28,720
Otherwise, display the value of x is not

369
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:30,560
three or five. This allows the program

370
00:14:28,720 --> 00:14:32,480
to choose what to do based on the value

371
00:14:30,560 --> 00:14:34,320
of x.

372
00:14:32,480 --> 00:14:36,399
Now, when you want to check one variable

373
00:14:34,320 --> 00:14:38,880
against several specific values, a

374
00:14:36,399 --> 00:14:41,519
switch statement is better.

375
00:14:38,880 --> 00:14:44,079
For example, if face is one, say roll

376
00:14:41,519 --> 00:14:46,560
the one. If face is two, say roll the

377
00:14:44,079 --> 00:14:49,279
two. Otherwise, say roll a number

378
00:14:46,560 --> 00:14:50,800
greater than or equal to three. This is

379
00:14:49,279 --> 00:14:53,040
easier to read than writing multiple of

380
00:14:50,800 --> 00:14:57,120
statements. And in mat lab, the switch

381
00:14:53,040 --> 00:14:59,199
ends automatically. No need for a break.

382
00:14:57,120 --> 00:15:01,120
Now, let's talk about vectorization. One

383
00:14:59,199 --> 00:15:02,880
of the most important habits in mat lab

384
00:15:01,120 --> 00:15:05,279
programming.

385
00:15:02,880 --> 00:15:07,279
Here's the idea. Instead of looping

386
00:15:05,279 --> 00:15:09,120
through each element one by one, we

387
00:15:07,279 --> 00:15:10,800
apply an operation to the whole array at

388
00:15:09,120 --> 00:15:13,760
once.

389
00:15:10,800 --> 00:15:16,720
For example, let's say x is the vector 1

390
00:15:13,760 --> 00:15:19,279
2 3. Using a loop, we would add five to

391
00:15:16,720 --> 00:15:22,720
each element one at a time. But with

392
00:15:19,279 --> 00:15:25,199
vectorization, we just say x= x + 5. And

393
00:15:22,720 --> 00:15:28,160
mat lab does it all at once. Same

394
00:15:25,199 --> 00:15:30,480
result, much faster, much cleaner.

395
00:15:28,160 --> 00:15:33,279
Whenever you can avoid loops, use

396
00:15:30,480 --> 00:15:35,760
vectorzed operations. Mat Lab is built

397
00:15:33,279 --> 00:15:37,920
for that.

398
00:15:35,760 --> 00:15:41,120
Let's look at how Mat Lab handles basic

399
00:15:37,920 --> 00:15:43,839
plots. Start by generating X values from

400
00:15:41,120 --> 00:15:46,560
minus1 to one using lindspace. Then take

401
00:15:43,839 --> 00:15:48,720
the sign of X to get Y.

402
00:15:46,560 --> 00:15:51,120
Use plot X and Y to create a basic line

403
00:15:48,720 --> 00:15:53,759
graph. To change the appearance, you can

404
00:15:51,120 --> 00:15:56,079
specify style and color. For example,

405
00:15:53,759 --> 00:15:58,639
plot XY black line draws the line in

406
00:15:56,079 --> 00:16:01,040
black. Use hold on if you want to

407
00:15:58,639 --> 00:16:03,040
overlay another plot on the same figure.

408
00:16:01,040 --> 00:16:05,279
Use figure to open a new figure window

409
00:16:03,040 --> 00:16:07,440
for a separate plot.

410
00:16:05,279 --> 00:16:11,600
Plotting helps visualize data and it's

411
00:16:07,440 --> 00:16:13,519
built into mat lab as a core strength.

412
00:16:11,600 --> 00:16:16,320
Sometimes you want to show several plots

413
00:16:13,519 --> 00:16:18,800
in one window. Use the subplot command

414
00:16:16,320 --> 00:16:21,680
to divide the figure into a grid. For

415
00:16:18,800 --> 00:16:23,920
example, subplot 2 3 1 creates two rows

416
00:16:21,680 --> 00:16:25,519
and three columns and selects the first

417
00:16:23,920 --> 00:16:27,360
cell.

418
00:16:25,519 --> 00:16:29,759
Each subplot acts like an independent

419
00:16:27,360 --> 00:16:32,800
plot. You can add titles, labels, and

420
00:16:29,759 --> 00:16:34,720
style just like a regular figure.

421
00:16:32,800 --> 00:16:36,880
This is helpful when comparing outputs

422
00:16:34,720 --> 00:16:39,600
or organizing results cleanly on a

423
00:16:36,880 --> 00:16:42,480
single screen.

424
00:16:39,600 --> 00:16:44,959
Now, let's add a third dimension.

425
00:16:42,480 --> 00:16:47,360
Use plot 3 of XYZ to make a 3D line

426
00:16:44,959 --> 00:16:49,120
plot. For surfaces, use the mesh

427
00:16:47,360 --> 00:16:52,240
command. It draws a grid over the

428
00:16:49,120 --> 00:16:54,639
surface defined by Z values.

429
00:16:52,240 --> 00:16:57,199
If you want a contour plot, use contour

430
00:16:54,639 --> 00:16:58,959
Z matrix that shows level curves of a

431
00:16:57,199 --> 00:17:00,720
surface.

432
00:16:58,959 --> 00:17:03,040
You can also control the axis limits

433
00:17:00,720 --> 00:17:05,039
with axis. Add a title with title and

434
00:17:03,040 --> 00:17:07,039
label the axis using X label and Y

435
00:17:05,039 --> 00:17:08,480
label.

436
00:17:07,039 --> 00:17:12,199
To explain the meaning of different

437
00:17:08,480 --> 00:17:12,199
curves, use legend.

438
00:17:12,640 --> 00:17:17,199
Here are a few examples of what you can

439
00:17:14,559 --> 00:17:19,120
do with mat lab plots.

440
00:17:17,199 --> 00:17:22,160
You'll see grouped bar charts, stacked

441
00:17:19,120 --> 00:17:24,799
bars, 2D lines, and 3D surfaces, all

442
00:17:22,160 --> 00:17:26,720
using basic commands.

443
00:17:24,799 --> 00:17:29,440
Try different styles, colors, and

444
00:17:26,720 --> 00:17:31,760
layouts to get the look you want. Good

445
00:17:29,440 --> 00:17:33,679
visualization tells a story, not just

446
00:17:31,760 --> 00:17:35,840
numbers, but patterns, relationships,

447
00:17:33,679 --> 00:17:37,840
and insights.

448
00:17:35,840 --> 00:17:41,120
Use plots not just to check results, but

449
00:17:37,840 --> 00:17:42,480
to communicate them clearly.

450
00:17:41,120 --> 00:17:44,799
Now, let's talk about reading and

451
00:17:42,480 --> 00:17:47,600
writing data files.

452
00:17:44,799 --> 00:17:50,480
To open a file, use fop and pass the

453
00:17:47,600 --> 00:17:53,760
file name and read mode. For example, f

454
00:17:50,480 --> 00:17:55,760
open in dot RT. This gives you a file ID

455
00:17:53,760 --> 00:17:57,679
or a FID.

456
00:17:55,760 --> 00:17:59,919
Use fcan F to read numbers from the

457
00:17:57,679 --> 00:18:02,320
file. When you're done, always close the

458
00:17:59,919 --> 00:18:04,400
file using F close. If you need

459
00:18:02,320 --> 00:18:07,600
formatting help, use help text read or

460
00:18:04,400 --> 00:18:09,679
help fint. Below is an example of a data

461
00:18:07,600 --> 00:18:11,919
file. It has names, types, numbers, and

462
00:18:09,679 --> 00:18:13,520
yes or no labels.

463
00:18:11,919 --> 00:18:15,440
You'll often need to work with mixed

464
00:18:13,520 --> 00:18:17,840
data. So learning how to read from and

465
00:18:15,440 --> 00:18:20,559
write to files is essential in mat lab

466
00:18:17,840 --> 00:18:22,400
workflows.

467
00:18:20,559 --> 00:18:25,679
Let's now learn how to read data files

468
00:18:22,400 --> 00:18:27,840
in mat lab both fully and partially. To

469
00:18:25,679 --> 00:18:30,400
read an entire structured data set, you

470
00:18:27,840 --> 00:18:32,559
first open the file using fopen. Then

471
00:18:30,400 --> 00:18:35,760
use the text read function to extract

472
00:18:32,559 --> 00:18:38,559
data from all columns. Each data type in

473
00:18:35,760 --> 00:18:40,799
the file corresponds to a format symbol

474
00:18:38,559 --> 00:18:43,120
like percent s for strings, percent f

475
00:18:40,799 --> 00:18:44,880
for floatingoint numbers and percent d

476
00:18:43,120 --> 00:18:46,960
for integers.

477
00:18:44,880 --> 00:18:49,039
Mat lab assigns each column to a

478
00:18:46,960 --> 00:18:50,640
variable automatically.

479
00:18:49,039 --> 00:18:52,559
This works well when you need to load

480
00:18:50,640 --> 00:18:55,919
the whole data set into memory like

481
00:18:52,559 --> 00:18:57,760
names, types, values, and so on.

482
00:18:55,919 --> 00:19:00,320
Now, if you only want to read part of

483
00:18:57,760 --> 00:19:02,000
the data, say just the first column, you

484
00:19:00,320 --> 00:19:04,240
can still use text read, but with

485
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:06,720
asterisks added to the format string to

486
00:19:04,240 --> 00:19:08,480
skip over the columns you don't need.

487
00:19:06,720 --> 00:19:10,400
Mat Lab will still move through the file

488
00:19:08,480 --> 00:19:13,200
correctly, but it'll only save the data

489
00:19:10,400 --> 00:19:15,200
you asked for. This selective reading is

490
00:19:13,200 --> 00:19:18,960
great for reducing memory usage or

491
00:19:15,200 --> 00:19:20,799
focusing on relevant features.

492
00:19:18,960 --> 00:19:23,360
Sometimes you don't need the full file,

493
00:19:20,799 --> 00:19:25,440
you just want a specific line. Maybe

494
00:19:23,360 --> 00:19:27,600
line two contains Joe's entry and that's

495
00:19:25,440 --> 00:19:29,520
all you care about.

496
00:19:27,600 --> 00:19:32,000
In this case, you can tell mat lab to

497
00:19:29,520 --> 00:19:34,080
skip one line and then read just one.

498
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:36,080
You still use text read, but now with

499
00:19:34,080 --> 00:19:37,840
two extra options, the number of lines

500
00:19:36,080 --> 00:19:39,760
to read and the number of header lines

501
00:19:37,840 --> 00:19:41,840
to skip.

502
00:19:39,760 --> 00:19:43,919
This lets you directly target a record

503
00:19:41,840 --> 00:19:46,559
without loading everything. Useful for

504
00:19:43,919 --> 00:19:50,240
index data or when you're debugging or

505
00:19:46,559 --> 00:19:52,240
sampling from a large file.

506
00:19:50,240 --> 00:19:54,799
So far, we've looked at reading files.

507
00:19:52,240 --> 00:19:56,720
Now let's switch to writing. To write

508
00:19:54,799 --> 00:19:59,840
data out, open a file in write mode

509
00:19:56,720 --> 00:20:01,919
using fop. Then use frrint f to format

510
00:19:59,840 --> 00:20:04,799
the output line, inserting variables in

511
00:20:01,919 --> 00:20:06,799
the order you want them saved.

512
00:20:04,799 --> 00:20:09,440
For example, you can save name, type,

513
00:20:06,799 --> 00:20:11,440
and numerical values all in one line.

514
00:20:09,440 --> 00:20:15,039
And as always, once writing is done,

515
00:20:11,440 --> 00:20:16,799
make sure to close the file with fclose.

516
00:20:15,039 --> 00:20:18,880
This gives you full control over the

517
00:20:16,799 --> 00:20:21,440
format, spacing, and layout of your

518
00:20:18,880 --> 00:20:23,280
data. Very useful when preparing results

519
00:20:21,440 --> 00:20:26,720
for review or generating output for

520
00:20:23,280 --> 00:20:28,640
other software to read.

521
00:20:26,720 --> 00:20:31,679
Next, let's see how to keep a record of

522
00:20:28,640 --> 00:20:34,559
your work using the diary command. Start

523
00:20:31,679 --> 00:20:36,320
with diary followed by a file name. From

524
00:20:34,559 --> 00:20:38,000
that point on, everything you type and

525
00:20:36,320 --> 00:20:40,720
everything MATLAB prints will be saved

526
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:44,000
in a log file. Once you're done, stop

527
00:20:40,720 --> 00:20:45,280
the recording by typing diary off.

528
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:47,440
This is great for tracking your

529
00:20:45,280 --> 00:20:49,280
workflow, recording sessions, or saving

530
00:20:47,440 --> 00:20:51,120
what you've done for future reference or

531
00:20:49,280 --> 00:20:53,600
reporting.

532
00:20:51,120 --> 00:20:55,039
Now, another useful tool is timing. If

533
00:20:53,600 --> 00:20:57,280
you want to measure how long your code

534
00:20:55,039 --> 00:20:59,760
takes to run, just put tick before the

535
00:20:57,280 --> 00:21:01,200
commands and talk after them. Mat Lab

536
00:20:59,760 --> 00:21:02,960
will show you the elapse time in

537
00:21:01,200 --> 00:21:04,559
seconds.

538
00:21:02,960 --> 00:21:08,000
This helps you compare different methods

539
00:21:04,559 --> 00:21:09,919
and optimize performance.

540
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:13,200
One of the key ideas in mat lab is that

541
00:21:09,919 --> 00:21:15,840
everything is a matrix. a single number

542
00:21:13,200 --> 00:21:18,240
that's just a one by one matrix. A row

543
00:21:15,840 --> 00:21:21,600
of numbers is a row vector. A column is

544
00:21:18,240 --> 00:21:23,919
a column vector. And an image that's a

545
00:21:21,600 --> 00:21:25,679
two-dimensional matrix. If it's in

546
00:21:23,919 --> 00:21:28,720
color, it becomes three-dimensional

547
00:21:25,679 --> 00:21:30,960
width, height, and color channels.

548
00:21:28,720 --> 00:21:33,360
This matrix mindset makes mat lab

549
00:21:30,960 --> 00:21:35,039
powerful and consistent across tasks,

550
00:21:33,360 --> 00:21:37,600
whether you're doing math, image

551
00:21:35,039 --> 00:21:41,039
processing, or even machine learning.

552
00:21:37,600 --> 00:21:44,240
Now, let's talk about indexing.

553
00:21:41,039 --> 00:21:47,039
If you have a matrix A, A of I, comma, J

554
00:21:44,240 --> 00:21:50,559
gives the element in row I in column J.

555
00:21:47,039 --> 00:21:54,000
A of I comma gives the entire IT row. A

556
00:21:50,559 --> 00:21:56,000
of comma J gives the whole JT column.

557
00:21:54,000 --> 00:21:58,080
You can reshape matrices, slice out

558
00:21:56,000 --> 00:22:00,640
parts or use logical masks to filter

559
00:21:58,080 --> 00:22:03,280
data. To modify the matrix, use

560
00:22:00,640 --> 00:22:06,159
expressions like A + 5, which adds five

561
00:22:03,280 --> 00:22:08,799
to every element or a dot star B for

562
00:22:06,159 --> 00:22:11,360
element byelement multiplication.

563
00:22:08,799 --> 00:22:14,960
This is the foundation of mat lab matrix

564
00:22:11,360 --> 00:22:17,360
thinking in everything you do

565
00:22:14,960 --> 00:22:19,440
in mat lab you can do both matrix and

566
00:22:17,360 --> 00:22:22,559
elementwise operations and the

567
00:22:19,440 --> 00:22:24,720
difference is all in the dot a times b

568
00:22:22,559 --> 00:22:26,400
with a single star means matrix

569
00:22:24,720 --> 00:22:29,280
multiplication

570
00:22:26,400 --> 00:22:31,679
a dot star b with a dot means multiply

571
00:22:29,280 --> 00:22:34,240
element by element the same goes for

572
00:22:31,679 --> 00:22:37,039
powers and division a corette 2 in

573
00:22:34,240 --> 00:22:39,679
raises the matrix to a power a dot

574
00:22:37,039 --> 00:22:41,840
corette 2 in squares each element. And

575
00:22:39,679 --> 00:22:44,080
for division, a divided by b solves

576
00:22:41,840 --> 00:22:46,720
equations, while a dot/b divides

577
00:22:44,080 --> 00:22:49,039
elementwise. These small differences are

578
00:22:46,720 --> 00:22:52,400
key when working with data equations or

579
00:22:49,039 --> 00:22:54,400
signals in mat lab.

580
00:22:52,400 --> 00:22:57,280
Whenever you can, avoid loops and write

581
00:22:54,400 --> 00:22:59,520
in matrix form. Instead of looping to

582
00:22:57,280 --> 00:23:04,080
square elements, just write a dot carrot

583
00:22:59,520 --> 00:23:07,440
2. Want row-wise sums? Use sum of a,

584
00:23:04,080 --> 00:23:09,600
two. Need probabilities per row? Divide

585
00:23:07,440 --> 00:23:11,840
each row by its total using matrix

586
00:23:09,600 --> 00:23:14,960
broadcasting. It's cleaner and faster

587
00:23:11,840 --> 00:23:16,640
than nested loops.

588
00:23:14,960 --> 00:23:20,240
The peaks function creates a sample

589
00:23:16,640 --> 00:23:22,000
surface. Use mesh to visualize it. To

590
00:23:20,240 --> 00:23:24,240
smooth it out, create a finer grid with

591
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:27,360
mesh grid and use interp 2 to estimate

592
00:23:24,240 --> 00:23:29,440
new Z values. This technique is useful

593
00:23:27,360 --> 00:23:31,600
in graphics, simulations, and image

594
00:23:29,440 --> 00:23:34,960
processing anytime you want smoother,

595
00:23:31,600 --> 00:23:37,120
higher resolution data.

596
00:23:34,960 --> 00:23:40,000
Here's how we morph faces using matrix

597
00:23:37,120 --> 00:23:42,240
tools. We warp two face images using

598
00:23:40,000 --> 00:23:44,400
interpolation on each color channel,

599
00:23:42,240 --> 00:23:46,960
then blend them using a weighted average

600
00:23:44,400 --> 00:23:49,520
with alpha controlling the mix. To

601
00:23:46,960 --> 00:23:51,360
detect unusual pixels, we reshape the

602
00:23:49,520 --> 00:23:54,000
image and compute the Mahalobi's

603
00:23:51,360 --> 00:23:56,320
distance from a reference color. Next,

604
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:58,320
we create a binary mask by thresholding

605
00:23:56,320 --> 00:24:01,039
the distance and apply a median filter

606
00:23:58,320 --> 00:24:03,360
to clean it up. It's a compact example

607
00:24:01,039 --> 00:24:05,200
of how mat lab combines image warping,

608
00:24:03,360 --> 00:24:08,000
statistics, and filtering all with

609
00:24:05,200 --> 00:24:09,840
matrices.

610
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:12,880
This example shows how plot resolution

611
00:24:09,840 --> 00:24:14,799
affects curve smoothness. In a loop, we

612
00:24:12,880 --> 00:24:18,480
increase the number of x values using

613
00:24:14,799 --> 00:24:20,080
lindspace, then compute y = x over 1 +

614
00:24:18,480 --> 00:24:22,320
x^2.

615
00:24:20,080 --> 00:24:24,080
We plot each result in a subplot grid

616
00:24:22,320 --> 00:24:26,880
with labels and titles showing the

617
00:24:24,080 --> 00:24:29,039
resolution. With each pause, you can see

618
00:24:26,880 --> 00:24:31,120
the graph getting smoother. A great way

619
00:24:29,039 --> 00:24:33,760
to visualize the impact of sampling

620
00:24:31,120 --> 00:24:35,279
density.

621
00:24:33,760 --> 00:24:37,360
Let's talk about the two main types of

622
00:24:35,279 --> 00:24:40,799
files you'll create in mat lab. Scripts

623
00:24:37,360 --> 00:24:42,240
and functions. Scripts are simple. They

624
00:24:40,799 --> 00:24:44,720
run a series of commands and use

625
00:24:42,240 --> 00:24:46,320
variables already in your workspace. You

626
00:24:44,720 --> 00:24:48,400
