627
00:24:46,320 --> 00:24:50,960
return anything out. They're great for

628
00:24:48,400 --> 00:24:52,799
quick experiments or automation.

629
00:24:50,960 --> 00:24:54,880
Functions, on the other hand, are more

630
00:24:52,799 --> 00:24:57,840
structured. You give them inputs and

631
00:24:54,880 --> 00:25:00,320
they return outputs. Inside a function,

632
00:24:57,840 --> 00:25:02,320
all variables are local, meaning they

633
00:25:00,320 --> 00:25:04,080
don't interfere with anything else in

634
00:25:02,320 --> 00:25:05,679
your workspace.

635
00:25:04,080 --> 00:25:07,440
Knowing when to use a script or a

636
00:25:05,679 --> 00:25:10,320
function is key to writing clean,

637
00:25:07,440 --> 00:25:12,320
reliable code.

638
00:25:10,320 --> 00:25:14,000
Here's a basic function example, one

639
00:25:12,320 --> 00:25:16,640
that calculates the area and

640
00:25:14,000 --> 00:25:18,320
circumference of a circle. We define a

641
00:25:16,640 --> 00:25:20,960
function called circle that takes one

642
00:25:18,320 --> 00:25:23,919
input, the radius. Inside we calculate

643
00:25:20,960 --> 00:25:27,120
the area as pi * radius squared and the

644
00:25:23,919 --> 00:25:30,159
circumference as 2 *<unk> * the radius.

645
00:25:27,120 --> 00:25:31,840
Save this in a file named circle.mm.

646
00:25:30,159 --> 00:25:34,480
Now to use this function, we write a

647
00:25:31,840 --> 00:25:36,480
script. For example, if the radius is 7,

648
00:25:34,480 --> 00:25:39,279
we call the function with that value and

649
00:25:36,480 --> 00:25:42,080
display the result using disp. You'd

650
00:25:39,279 --> 00:25:43,760
save this script as my script.mm.

651
00:25:42,080 --> 00:25:46,159
This is how functions and scripts work

652
00:25:43,760 --> 00:25:49,919
together. You build reusable tools with

653
00:25:46,159 --> 00:25:51,840
functions and run them from scripts.

654
00:25:49,919 --> 00:25:55,760
Let's try a simple numerical task.

655
00:25:51,840 --> 00:25:58,080
Summing 1 / i^2 from i= 1 to 10. We can

656
00:25:55,760 --> 00:26:01,679
do this two ways. Forward starting from

657
00:25:58,080 --> 00:26:03,600
i= 1 or backwards starting from i= 10.

658
00:26:01,679 --> 00:26:06,880
The code is nearly identical just with

659
00:26:03,600 --> 00:26:08,960
the loop going in reverse. Now in theory

660
00:26:06,880 --> 00:26:10,640
both results should be the same. But

661
00:26:08,960 --> 00:26:13,200
because of floatingoint rounding the

662
00:26:10,640 --> 00:26:15,120
order can make a small difference. This

663
00:26:13,200 --> 00:26:16,880
is a great example of how numerical

664
00:26:15,120 --> 00:26:20,080
stability matters, especially in

665
00:26:16,880 --> 00:26:21,760
scientific computing.

666
00:26:20,080 --> 00:26:23,919
Now, let's write our own matrix

667
00:26:21,760 --> 00:26:26,400
multiplication function. No built-in

668
00:26:23,919 --> 00:26:29,279
functions allowed. We create a function

669
00:26:26,400 --> 00:26:32,480
